  height: 330,
};

const foundationsCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Newton\'s second law', note: 'F = ma. The whole of dynamics is this equation applied honestly: find the forces, find the motion.' },
    { math: '2 · Work and energy', note: 'Work = force × distance. Integrating F = ma over distance gives the work–energy theorem: net work equals the change in kinetic energy.' },
    { math: '3 · Conservation when forces are friendly', note: 'Gravity and springs store work reversibly, so kinetic + potential energy stays constant. Friction is the tax collector.' },
    { math: '4 · Momentum for collisions', note: 'Impulse = force × time. Over a collision, momentum is conserved even when energy is not — that is why crumple zones work.' },
    { math: '5 · Rigid bodies add rotation', note: 'The same laws, rotated: torque replaces force, moment of inertia replaces mass, ω replaces v. Every machine is this chapter.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/mechanical-engineering/foundational/mechanics-foundations">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Mechanical engineering is Newtonian mechanics put to work: bodies, forces and motion, with
    heat and materials added on top. Its foundations are small enough to write on a postcard —
    force equals mass times acceleration, work equals force times distance, impulse equals force
    times time — and deep enough that gearboxes, cranes, engine cranks and wind turbines are all
    consequences of those few lines. The subject is three centuries old and has not needed a
    correction inside everyday engineering: if your mechanism prediction fails, the laws are not
    wrong, your force inventory is.
  </p>
  <p>
    What separates a competent engineer from a formula-matcher is knowing that the different
    "laws" are not really different subjects. They are one law — Newton's second law — seen from
    three useful angles. Integrate it over distance and you get the work–energy theorem, which
    answers "how fast?" questions without caring about the path. Integrate it over time and you
    get impulse–momentum, which answers collision questions without needing to know the
    terrifyingly large forces inside the impact. Rotate the whole apparatus and it describes
    spinning shafts as cleanly as sliding blocks. A large fraction of mechanical engineering
    analysis is choosing the angle that turns a ten-line problem into a one-line problem.
  </p>
  <p>
    This chapter builds those three angles in order. First, forces as vectors — the bookkeeping
    that keeps directions honest. Then motion itself, kinematics, and its marriage to forces,
    kinetics. Finally the two integrals — work–energy and impulse–momentum — that make hard
    problems easy, and the rigid-body versions that let the theory drive real machines.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the chain from F = ma to machines" widget="equationStep" config={foundationsCfg} />

  <h2>Vector mechanics: forces with directions</h2>
  <p>
    A force is not a number. It has a size, a line of action and a sense — pull or push — and
    forgetting any of the three produces an answer that is exactly, confidently wrong. Vector
    mechanics is the discipline of keeping directions honest: resolve every force into
    perpendicular components, apply equilibrium (sums of forces and moments equal zero) to each
    component separately, and let the geometry carry the physics.
  </p>
  <p>
    A worked example shows the payoff. A gardener drags a 40 kg loaded sledge with a rope held
    at 30 degrees above horizontal, tension 200 N. Only the horizontal component does the
    pulling: 200 × cos 30° ≈ 173 N. The vertical component, 200 × sin 30° = 100 N, pulls
    <em>upward</em>, so the ground reaction drops from 40 × 9.81 ≈ 392 N to about 292 N — the
    upward pull lightens the load and shrinks its friction. The friction force, at a
    coefficient of 0.4, is roughly 0.4 × 292 ≈ 117 N, leaving a net 56 N to accelerate the
    sledge at about 1.4 m/s². Notice what happened: one trigonometric decomposition turned a
    muddy real situation into three clean numbers, and revealed a subtlety (angle reduces
    friction) that pure intuition misses.
  </p>
  <p>
    Vectors also add the concept of a <em>moment</em> — the turning effect of a force, its size
    times the perpendicular distance from the pivot. Moments explain why a long spanner loosens
    a bolt a short one cannot, why doorknobs live far from hinges, and why a crane's counterweight
    must sit far from the tower: the turning effect is force times lever arm, so distance buys
    as much leverage as weight. Equilibrium of any rigid body is three equations in two
    dimensions — two force balances and one moment balance — and every statics problem in the
    discipline is a disguise for those three.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> heavy objects fall faster. In the absence of air resistance,
    acceleration under gravity is 9.81 m/s² for every mass — Galileo's result, confirmed to
    extraordinary precision. Mass appears on both sides of F = ma and cancels. A bowling ball
    does hit the ground before a feather, but only because air drag decelerates the feather
    sooner relative to its weight, not because gravity pulls harder on it per kilogram.
  </p>

  <h2>Kinematics and kinetics of rigid bodies</h2>
  <p>
    <em>Kinematics</em> describes motion without asking what causes it: position, velocity,
    acceleration, and for rotation the angle θ, angular velocity ω and angular acceleration α.
    The vocabulary is worth learning exactly because it mirrors linear motion term for term —
    every linear equation has a rotational twin with angle in place of position. The bridge
    between the two worlds is v = ωr: a point on a spinning body at radius r moves with speed
    equal to angular velocity times radius. It is why the outer edge of a grinding wheel
    screams while its centre is silent, and why the tip of a wind-turbine blade travels
    around 250 km/h while the hub barely moves.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Kinetics</em> supplies the cause: Newton's second law, in rotational dress. Torque
    plays the role of force, and the moment of inertia I — the rotational analogue of mass,
    measuring how far the body's mass sits from the axis — plays the role of inertia: τ = Iα.
    Consider a flywheel of inertia 2 kg·m² driven by a net torque of 6 N·m: it spins up at
    α = 6 ÷ 2 = 3 rad/s², so from rest it reaches a working speed of 150 rad/s (about
    1,430 rpm) in 50 seconds. The energy now stored in it is ½Iω² = 0.5 × 2 × 150² = 22.5 kJ —
    a calculation an engineer can do in one line, and which sizing a real energy-storage
    flywheel extends directly.
  </p>
  <p>
    Rotation is where machines live. Gears are the kinematic promise "trade torque for speed"
    made hardware: mesh a 20-tooth pinion into a 60-tooth wheel and the wheel turns at one
    third the speed with three times the torque — the gear ratio — losing only friction.
    A car's gearbox is nothing but a switchable set of such ratios, keeping the engine near
    its efficient speed while the wheels cover every speed from parking to highway.
  </p>

  <h2>Work–energy: the power of not caring about the path</h2>
  <p>
    Newton's law answers "what happens next, instant by instant?" — powerful, but laborious
    when forces vary along the way. Integrating force over <em>distance</em> produces the
    work–energy theorem: the net work done on a body equals its change in kinetic energy,
    ½mv². The magic is that the path disappears. If you know only the start and end states,
    the theorem hands you the answer without a single intermediate step.
  </p>
  <p>
    The most safety-critical application is braking. A tyre gripping dry asphalt decelerates a
    car at about μg, roughly 0.7 × 9.81 ≈ 6.9 m/s². Skidding to a stop from 20 m/s takes
    v² ÷ (2μg) = 400 ÷ 13.7 ≈ 29 m. From 40 m/s the distance is 1600 ÷ 13.7 ≈ 117 m — four
    times, not twice, because kinetic energy grows with the <em>square</em> of speed. The same
    squaring sets crash severity: a 1,500 kg car at 50 km/h (about 13.9 m/s) carries
    ½ × 1500 × 13.9² ≈ 145 kJ, energy that something must absorb before the occupants stop
    moving.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="KE grows with v², PE with h — drag and compare" widget="plotter" config={energyCfg} />

  <p>
    When the only forces doing work are gravity and springs — "conservative" forces, which give
    back what they store — kinetic plus potential energy is conserved outright, and problems
    become comparisons of two bookkeeping entries. A roller-coaster car released from 40 m can
    reach, at best, √(2gh) = √(2 × 9.81 × 40) ≈ 28 m/s at the bottom; friction takes its tax
    from whatever remains. <em>Common misconception:</em> momentum and kinetic energy are
    interchangeable. They are not — momentum is mv and is a vector; kinetic energy is ½mv² and
    is a scalar. In a collision, momentum is essentially always conserved; kinetic energy is
    conserved only in ideal elastic impacts. Two lumps of clay that stick together conserve
    momentum exactly while converting most of their kinetic energy into heat and deformation —
    both statements are true simultaneously, and engineering crash analysis depends on holding
    both at once.
  </p>

  <h2>Impulse–momentum: mastering the collision</h2>
  <p>
    Integrate Newton's law over <em>time</em> instead of distance and you get impulse equals
    change in momentum: force × duration = mΔv. This is the right tool whenever huge forces act
    for short times — impact, propulsion, ball games — because you rarely know the peak force,
    but you can often know its integral. A crumple zone is impulse management made metal: the
    car must lose 13.9 m/s of velocity no matter what, so the design question is how much
    <em>time</em> and <em>distance</em> to spend doing it. Crush 0.5 m of structure and the
    average deceleration is v² ÷ (2s) ≈ 278 m/s², about 28 g; double the crush distance and
    the force halves. Every safety feature in a modern car — crumple zones, airbags, helmet
    foam — is an argument about stretching a deceleration over more time.
  </p>
  <p>
    In collisions between free bodies, total momentum is conserved even as energy converts to
    heat and sound, which lets engineers compute post-impact motion from pre-impact velocities
    alone. That single fact underlies accident reconstruction (from skid marks and final
    positions back to impact speeds), ballistics, and the docking of spacecraft, where a soft
    capture is defined as one that conserves momentum while dissipating the relative speed.
  </p>
  <p>
    Sport supplies the friendliest numbers. A pitched baseball arrives at 40 m/s and leaves the
    bat at 45 m/s the other way; the 0.145 kg ball's momentum change is 0.145 × (40 + 45) ≈
    12.3 kg·m/s. The bat and ball are in contact for about a millisecond, so the average force
    during contact is 12.3 ÷ 0.001 ≈ 12,000 newtons — the weight of a small car, applied for
    one thousandth of a second. Nobody "feels" a force that size as such; what the hitter feels
    is the impulse. This is also why follow-through matters in every bat-and-ball sport: it is
    a deliberate attempt to lengthen the contact time, buying more momentum change for the
    same peak force.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Rigid-body dynamics keeps a second conservation law in reserve: angular momentum, Iω, is
    conserved when no external torque acts. It explains the figure skater who pulls in her arms
    — I falls, so ω rises to keep the product fixed — and the reaction wheels that reorient
    spacecraft without thrusters. In machine analysis, the work–energy and impulse–momentum
    theorems extend naturally: a rolling body's kinetic energy splits between translation and
    rotation (½mv² + ½Iω²), which is why a hoop rolls slower than a solid disc released on the
    same slope — the disc stores less of its energy in spin.
  </p>
  <p>
    Further depth moves into machinery and vibration. D'Alembert's trick treats mass-times-
    acceleration as an inertial force, converting dynamics problems into statics ones — the
    conceptual basis of load cases in vehicle and crane design. Virtual work generalises the
    equilibrium idea to systems of linked bodies, and energy methods grow into Lagrange's
    equations, the standard tool for multi-degree-of-freedom mechanisms. Vibration enters when
    stiffness joins inertia: a spring–mass system has a natural frequency
    f = (1 ÷ 2π)√(k ÷ m), and rotating machinery is designed so its operating speed avoids
    that number — passing safely through it during startup, dwelling near it never. The open
    frontier is less the laws than the bookkeeping at scale: multibody simulation of
    hundred-part mechanisms, contact and friction models that remain stable, and digital
    twins that keep a Newtonian model synchronised with a real machine wearing out in the
    field.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> NCEES FE Mechanical exam specifications; Hibbeler, <em>Engineering Mechanics</em>; Meriam &amp; Kraige, <em>Dynamics</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
